{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310662,"nonce":"d6xb6PumKNd5Hn5BBmir2qA12Jo=","title":"`Std.parseInt` returns `Null\u003cInt\u003e` (boxed pointer). Assigning to a plain `Int` local via `var x:Int = nullInt;`, or via ternary `var x = ...","message":"`nue/examples/llama-chat/Main.hx`:\n```haxe\nvar ctxRaw = (args.length \u003e 3) ? Std.parseInt(args[3]) : null;  // Null\u003cInt\u003e\nvar ctx:Int = DEFAULT_CTX;\nif (ctxRaw != null) {\n    var unboxed:Int = ctxRaw;   // ← does NOT unbox; underlying reg is still Ptr\u003cInt\u003e\n    if (unboxed \u003e 0) ctx = unboxed;\n}\nloader.loadWithTokenizer(path, ctx);   // cross-file: arrives as 1416861664 etc.\n```\n\nCompare `loader.loadWithTokenizer(path, 4096)` (literal Int) — arrives correctly as 4096.\n\nCompare `trace(\"ctx=\" + ctx)` (same-file String concat) — prints `ctx=4096` correctly, because the `+` overload unboxes implicitly.\n\n\n```haxe\nvar ctxRaw = ...;\nvar ctx:Int = DEFAULT_CTX;\nif (ctxRaw != null) {\n    var unboxed = ctxRaw + 0;   // arithmetic forces unbox per bugs_null_arith_no_unbox fix\n    if (unboxed \u003e 0) ctx = unboxed;\n}\n```\n\nWith `+ 0`, `ctx` propagates correctly through the cross-file call and `meta.maxSeqLen=4096` lands as expected. Llama-chat memory drops from \u003e40 GB (uncapped 131072 context) to 4.4 GB (4096 context).\n\n\n1. `Std.parseInt(str)` returns `Null\u003cInt\u003e` — boxed `DynamicValue*`.\n2. `var unboxed:Int = ctxRaw` — TAST narrows the type but HIR→MIR doesn't insert `maybe_unbox_optional` because the destination is a typed-local assignment (not a binop, not a call-arg in the same file).\n3. Within the same file, the JIT often constant-folds or the inliner sees both ends and the issue is masked. Cross-file CallDirect passes the register-i64 as-is — Cranelift just moves the bits. The receiving function reads `maxCtx:Int` from the parameter slot and gets the pointer's low bits.\n\n\n1. **Typed-local assignment from `Null\u003cT\u003e` to `T` should insert an unbox**: extend `maybe_unbox_optional` in hir_to_mir to fire on `Let { ty: Int, init: \u003cNull\u003cInt\u003e expr\u003e }` and `Assign { lhs_ty: Int, rhs_ty: Null\u003cInt\u003e }`. Same logic that handles binop unbox already exists.\n2. **Cross-file call-arg coercion should unbox when callee expects unboxed primitive**: `coerce_args_for_cross_module_call` in hir_to_mir already exists for stdlib calls (see `bugs_int64_multivar_stub_replace`); extend it to detect `Optional{primitive} → primitive` coercions and emit the unbox before the CallDirect.\n\nEither fix removes the need for the `+ 0` workaround.\n\n\n- `nue/examples/llama-chat/Main.hx` — ctx unbox via `+ 0`.\n\nLikely affected, not yet hit:\n- Any code that passes a `Std.parseInt` result as an `Int` argument across a file boundary.\n- Any code that stores a `Null\u003cInt\u003e` in a same-file local and then passes that local to an imported function.\n\n\n- [[bugs-null-arith-no-unbox]] — the binop arm of this same problem; fixed by inserting unbox in arithmetic. The Let/Assign/Call-arg arms are the gap this memory documents.\n- [[bugs-int64-multivar-stub-replace]] — cross-module CallDirect renumbering; the coerce path mentioned in fix-2 above is the same coerce_args_for_cross_module_call code.\n- [[bugs-dtype-enum-cross-file-pointer]] — same shape (boxed value crossing file boundary, low byte truncates), but for enum variants. The compiler fix path is similar: type-driven coercion at the call/assign boundary.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_null_int_cross_file_assign` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}